## Existing SAU Implementation

- `src/sau/KuiSau.py`: Python SimObject definition.
- `src/sau/KuiSau.hh` and `src/sau/KuiSau.cc`: C++ SimObject, ports, CSR decode,
  memory requests, and timing events.
- `src/sau/KuiSauRun.hh` and `src/sau/KuiSauRun.cc`: lightweight systolic-array
  helper used by the current random ReadA/ReadB/Compute/Write path.
- `src/sau/CsrGen.py`, `src/sau/CsrGen.hh`, and `src/sau/CsrGen.cc`: fixed CSR
  request generator for integration testing.
- `src/sau/SauGoldenGen.py`, `src/sau/SauGoldenGen.hh`, and
`src/sau/SauGoldenGen.cc`: deterministic memory/CSR driver that checks
  parameterized 16x16 output vectors (`gemm`, `add`, `gemm_shift`,
  `gemm_dequant`, `gemm_dequant_negative`, `gemm_dequant_int16`,
  `pointwise`, `pointwise_dequant_mixed`, `gemm_out_transpose`,
  `normal_conv`, `normal_conv_rich`, `normal_conv_shift`, `normal_conv_stride`,
  `normal_conv_stride_shift`, `normal_conv_dequant_mixed`,
  `normal_conv_stride_dequant_mixed`,
  `normal_conv_stride_shift_dequant_mixed`,
  `depthwise_conv`, `depthwise_conv_rich`, `depthwise_conv_shift`,
  `depthwise_conv_stride`,
  `depthwise_conv_stride_shift`,
  `depthwise_conv_dequant_mixed`, `depthwise_conv_stride_dequant_mixed`,
  `depthwise_conv_stride_shift_dequant_mixed`,
  `gemm_retain`, `gemm_transpose_retain`).

The current `KuiSau::startup()` schedules a standalone random memory FSM through
`sendOneKuiPkt()` only when `enable_random_traffic=True`. CSR-triggered
execution exists through `processCsrPacket()` and `executeFlow()`. The flow now
waits for tagged A/B/C memory responses before compute and waits for final D
write response before completing the flow. The random memory FSM has an
in-flight guard and advances from untagged read responses, so it remains a
connectivity smoke path without flooding memory with repeated reads. `KuiSau`
registers a proper gem5 memory requestor id through `System`; avoid reintroducing
fixed numeric requestor ids for memory traffic.

The functional data path has started converging toward `SAU.py`: A/B memory
responses are treated as byte lanes, C is packed as software-style `uint16`
bias data, preprocessing includes `fliplr`, int8 sign extension, shift-mode
int16 view/reshape, convolution A shift, depthwise B masking, transpose mode,
and fixed A transpose before systolic compute. Bias add, dequant saturation,
output transpose, and output byte packing have also been ported closer to the
software model. Deterministic GEMM, matrix-add, shift-mode GEMM,
positive/negative/int16 cutbit/dequant GEMM, pointwise-convolution+bias,
pointwise mixed-sign dequant, output-transpose,
normal-convolution, normal-convolution shift-mode int16 output packing,
normal-convolution rich signed-data,
normal-convolution stride-mode output row selection,
normal-convolution combined stride+shift int16 output packing,
normal-convolution mixed-sign dequant,
normal-convolution combined stride+dequant,
normal-convolution combined stride+shift+dequant,
depthwise-convolution, depthwise-convolution shift-mode int16 output packing,
depthwise-convolution rich signed-data,
depthwise-convolution stride-mode output row selection,
depthwise-convolution combined stride+shift int16 output packing,
depthwise-convolution mixed-sign dequant,
depthwise-convolution combined stride+dequant,
depthwise-convolution combined stride+shift+dequant,
output-retain, and
output-transpose-retain tests now pass. The model no longer resets `D_matrix` on every start
write; this preserves the accumulated D state needed by `flow_mode == 2/3`
until `updateOutputMatrix()` decides whether to clear it. A/B/C reads are issued
as tagged per-segment requests using the software
`base + i * step + j * unitSize` pattern and reassembled by segment index. D
writeback is tagged per output row and busy clears only after all write
responses. Basic gem5 stats now count CSR reads/writes, flow starts/completions,
tagged read/write segments, busy ticks, per-kind tagged address summaries
(`first`, `last`, `sum`, `xor`) for MatrixA, MatrixB, VectorC, and OutputD, and
a global tagged request summary including an order-sensitive 53-bit hash.
Full functional equivalence is still not proven; the next major gaps are
additional normal/depthwise modes and data shapes, richer mixed-mode
shift/dequant variants, tighter D_wstrb coverage, and broader RTL
request-trace validation.

The latest completed slice adds normal/depthwise convolution golden vectors.
`normal_conv` uses `conv_kernel=2`, `register_mode=0`, `shift_mode=0`, one
nonzero A lane, zero C/bias data, and the fixed B row pattern; its expected D
bytes have only row 0 populated with `0..15` after SAU.py-style output packing.
`depthwise_conv` uses the same A/C setup with `register_mode=2`; its expected D
bytes have only the last byte of row 0 set to 15 after the DW B-column mask and
output packing.
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev scons build/RISCV/gem5.opt -j2`
passed after adding these cases.
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_normal_conv ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_normal_conv_test.py`
and
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_depthwise_conv ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_depthwise_conv_test.py`
passed. Both stats shapes show 8 CSR writes, 11 CSR reads, 1 flow
start/completion, 10 read segments (4 A + 4 B + 2 C), 16 D write segments, and
177001 busy ticks.

Latest completed slice: normal/depthwise rich-data convolution now passes.
`SauGoldenGen(test_case="normal_conv_rich")` and
`SauGoldenGen(test_case="depthwise_conv_rich")` both use `conv_kernel=2`,
`shift_mode=0`, zero C data, multiple signed A lanes across the six conv read
rows, and distinct signed B kernel rows. The normal-conv vector produces
SAU.py-derived mixed positive/negative packed bytes across output rows 0..4,
proving more than a one-hot A-to-B-row selection. The depthwise vector reuses the
same A/B data under `register_mode=2` and proves the DW B-column mask keeps five
output rows while zeroing the other packed columns. Verified commands:
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_normal_conv_rich ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_normal_conv_rich_test.py`
and
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_depthwise_conv_rich ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_depthwise_conv_rich_test.py`.
Both rich-data stats: 8 CSR writes, 11 CSR reads, 1 flow start/completion,
10 read segments, 16 D write segments, and 177001 busy ticks.

Latest completed slice: tagged address summary stats now exist and are verified
on the normal/depthwise rich-data conv vectors. `KuiSau` records first, last,
sum, and XOR address summaries for MatrixA reads, MatrixB reads, VectorC reads,
and OutputD writes. The verified rich-data conv address sequence is:
A reads `0x20000000..0x20000030` every 16 bytes, B reads
`0x20001000..0x20001030`, C reads `0x20002000` and `0x20002010`, and D writes
`0x20003000..0x200030f0` every 16 bytes. Verified commands:
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev scons build/RISCV/gem5.opt -j2`,
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_normal_conv_rich ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_normal_conv_rich_test.py`,
and
`docker exec -w /gem5/gem5-kui gem5-dev ./build/RISCV/gem5.opt --outdir=m5out/sau_golden_depthwise_conv_rich ./configs/tutorial/part1/kui_sau_golden_depthwise_conv_rich_test.py`.
Both runs matched expected stats summaries: A first/last/sum/xor
`536870912/536870960/2147483744/0`, B
`536875008/536875056/2147500128/0`, C
`536879104/536879120/1073758224/16`, and D
`536883200/536883440/8590133120/0`.

