`m5out/sau_order_gemm_shift_rtl_cgate` reports
`80/536870912/536883696/42950149504/0/3684773384763523`.
These values matched an independent address-sequence checker.

Latest completed slice: a reusable trace-summary checker now exists at
`util/sau_trace_summary.py`. It uses the same request-kind values and 53-bit
order hash as `KuiSau`, knows the RTL-gated representative cases
(`normal_conv_stride`, `normal_conv_stride_shift`, `pointwise`, and
`gemm_shift`), checks gem5 `stats.txt`, and can summarize RTL CSV/text traces.
For RTL CSV, either provide explicit `kind/source` plus `addr`, or provide
`sram_rd_enable`, `sram_wr_enable`, `sram_mem_addr`, `input_switch`, and
`bias_rd_valid`; the tool maps RTL vertical=A, horizontal=B, bias=C, writes=D.
Verified commands:
`python3 -m py_compile util/sau_trace_summary.py`,
`python3 util/sau_trace_summary.py --case normal_conv_stride --gem5-stats m5out/sau_order_normal_conv_stride_rtl_cgate/stats.txt`,
`python3 util/sau_trace_summary.py --case normal_conv_stride_shift --gem5-stats m5out/sau_order_normal_conv_stride_shift_rtl_cgate/stats.txt`,
`python3 util/sau_trace_summary.py --case pointwise --gem5-stats m5out/sau_order_pointwise_rtl_cgate/stats.txt`, and
`python3 util/sau_trace_summary.py --case gemm_shift --gem5-stats m5out/sau_order_gemm_shift_rtl_cgate/stats.txt`.

